// Cluster configuration for a matmul testbench system.
//
// M=8, N=2, K=32  ->  Bc = M*meshRow = 128, Br = N*meshCol = 32, d = K*tileSize = 128,
// at mesh 16x4x16. The TILE is unchanged; only how it maps onto the larger mesh is.
//
// These are SHAPE 1 only: S^T = K.Q^T, so M*meshRow = Bc and K*tileSize = d.
//
// Bc and d used to be forced equal, because that made both matmuls the same shape and let one
// streamer config drive both. They are independent now -- shape 2 (O^T = V^T.P^T, M*meshRow = d,
// K*tileSize = Bc) is generated into data_shape2.h by gen_shape2.sh and switched in per
// dispatch. So d is set by the MODEL and Bc is the tiling knob, free to fill TCDM.
//
// MEASURED at d = 128, computeLanes = 16: Bc 128 -> 512 takes the mesh from 47.7% to 64.2% of
// peak (14.68 -> 10.91 cycles per key) because the fixed per-tile costs amortise over 4x the
// beats. Keep NKV >= 4 so the software pipeline still hides the per-tile fill and drain.
//
// M and N are swapped relative to the untransposed form because FlashAttention here is
// computed TRANSPOSED:
// S^T = (Q.K^T)^T = K.Q^T is the same GEMM with its operands swapped, so
// D = [M*meshRow, N*meshCol] = [64, 32] = [Bc, Br]. That orientation is what
// lets StreamReduce's LANEWISE mode do rowmax with no horizontal fold -- one
// lane is one query row, so the reduction runs along beats. See snax-flashattn.c.
{
  // gemm configurations
  transposed_A: 0,
  transposed_B: 0,
  K: 32,
  N: 2,
  M: 32,
  bypassSIMD: 1,
  // broadcast_C won't work in the serial C input
  broadcast_C: 0,
  channel_en_C: 1,
}
